v Box”也会推出。售价5999美元。外形是个黑盒子。AI计算非常快。里面装了给开发者用的Windows 11。", "Windows 11的Copilot要改版了。分成三个标签：“Home”“Code”和“Autopilot”。在Code里，没写过代码的人也能做应用。在Autopilot里，可以看AI智能体干活。", "新的搜索也要来了。在搜索栏里就能直接改屏幕设置等。", "微软还发布了叫“MXC”的安全机制。它把AI智能体的活动关在盒子里。公司可以决定每个智能体能看什么。", "微软把这叫“混合智能”。AI不在云端，而是在电脑里运行。安全和好不好用，还是有人担心。"], "body": "マイクロソフトは新しい「Surface Laptop Ultra」を発表しました。値段は2599ドルからです。Nvidiaの新しいチップを使っています。メモリは多く、ゲームもAIの仕事もできます。10月16日から売り始めます。\n\n「RTX Spark Dev Box」も出ます。値段は5999ドルです。黒い箱の形をしています。AIの計算がとても速いです。中には開発者向けのWindows 11が入っています。\n\nWindows 11のCopilotが新しくなります。タブが3つになります。「Home」と「Code」と「Autopilot」です。Codeでは、コードを書いたことがない人もアプリを作れます。Autopilotでは、AIエージェントの仕事を見ます。\n\n新しい検索も来ます。検索バーから、画面の設定などを直接変えられます。\n\nマイクロソフトは「MXC」という安全の仕組みも発表しました。AIエージェントの動きを箱の中だけにします。会社はエージェントが何を見られるか決められます。\n\nマイクロソフトは「ハイブリッド・インテリジェンス」と言っています。クラウドではなく、パソコンの中でAIを動かします。安全や使いやすさの心配はまだあります。", "body_zh": "微软发布了新的“Surface Laptop Ultra”。售价2599美元起。它用了英伟达的新芯片。内存很大，玩游戏和做AI工作都行。10月16日开始卖。\n\n“RTX Spark Dev Box”也会推出。售价5999美元。外形是个黑盒子。AI计算非常快。里面装了给开发者用的Windows 11。\n\nWindows 11的Copilot要改版了。分成三个标签：“Home”“Code”和“Autopilot”。在Code里，没写过代码的人也能做应用。在Autopilot里，可以看AI智能体干活。\n\n新的搜索也要来了。在搜索栏里就能直接改屏幕设置等。\n\n微软还发布了叫“MXC”的安全机制。它把AI智能体的活动关在盒子里。公司可以决定每个智能体能看什么。\n\n微软把这叫“混合智能”。AI不在云端，而是在电脑里运行。安全和好不好用，还是有人担心。", "text": "マイクロソフトは新しい「Surface Laptop Ultra」を発表しました。値段は2599ドルからです。Nvidiaの新しいチップを使っています。メモリは多く、ゲームもAIの仕事もできます。10月16日から売り始めます。 「RTX Spark Dev Box」も出ます。値段は5999ドルです。黒い箱の形をしています。AIの計算がとても速いです。中には開発者向けのWindows 11が入っています。 Windows 11のCopilotが新しくなります。タブが3つになります。「Home」と「Code」と「Autopilot」です。Codeでは、コードを書いたことがない人もアプリを作れます。Autopilotでは、AIエージェントの仕事を見ます。 新しい検索も来ます。検索バーから、画面の設定などを直接変えられます。 マイクロソフトは「MXC」という安全の仕組みも発表しました。AIエージェントの動きを箱の中だけにします。会社はエージェントが何を見られるか決められます。 マイクロソフトは「ハイブリッド・インテリジェンス」と言っています。クラウドではなく、パソコンの中でAIを動かします。安全や使いやすさの心配はまだあります。", "vocab": [{"word": "発表", "pos": "noun", "zh": "发布；宣布", "note": "常和「する」一起用：発表する"}, {"word": "値段", "pos": "noun", "zh": "价格", "note": "「ねだん」と読む"}, {"word": "使う", "pos": "verb", "zh": "使用", "note": "五段动词，て形是「使って」"}, {"word": "売る", "pos": "verb", "zh": "卖", "note": "「売り始める」＝开始卖"}, {"word": "変わる", "pos": "verb", "zh": "改变", "note": "自动词，他动词是「変える」"}, {"word": "新しい", "pos": "adj", "zh": "新的", "note": "い形容词"}, {"word": "安全", "pos": "noun", "zh": "安全", "note": "「あんぜん」と読む"}, {"word": "仕事", "pos": "noun", "zh": "工作", "note": "「しごと」と読む"}, {"word": "仕組み", "pos": "noun", "zh": "机制；结构", "note": "「しくみ」と読む"}, {"word": "心配", "pos": "noun", "zh": "担心", "note": "「しんぱい」と読む；心配する＝担心"}], "title_original": "Microsoft event debuts new AI-friendly hardware and Windows changes", "summary_original": "Get ready for more AI in your Windows and more AI on the desktop.", "body_original": "Hybrid Intelligence\nMicrosoft event debuts new AI-friendly hardware and Windows changes\nGet ready for more AI in your Windows and more AI on the desktop.\nNick Indge\n–\nOct 7, 2026 8:00 pm\n|\n65\nThe Surface Laptop Ultra\nCredit:\nMicrosoft\nThe Surface Laptop Ultra\nCredit:\nMicrosoft\nText\nsettings\nStory text\nSize\nSmall\nStandard\nLarge\nWidth\n*\nStandard\nWide\nLinks\nStandard\nOrange\n* Subscribers only\nLearn more\nMinimize to nav\nIn its first live event in two years, Microsoft today announced its newest Surface laptop, outlined a host of changes coming to Windows 11, and shared a vision of how local-AI and agentic workflows could reshape personal computing for those in the dev community as well as home enthusiasts.\nSurface Laptop Ultra\nOriginally teased in May 2026\n, the new Surface Laptop Ultra will be Microsoft’s first offering to take advantage of the new Nvidia RTX Spark SoC (system on a chip). The device has a starting price of $2,599, and comes in various SoC and memory configurations, with either a 5120-core or 6144-core Blackwell GPU and up to 128GB of LPDDR5x unified memory. It will begin shipping on October 16 and is available for\npre-order\nnow.\nWhile the new Ultra is clearly focused on the developer crowd, graphical performance was highlighted during the presentation to show off just how capable the laptop can be. By leaning on unified memory allocation rather than the physical VRAM limits of a traditional GPU, the laptop can assign its resources to handle modern gaming tasks as well as local AI development and deployment. The new\nGears of War: E-Day\nwas shown running on the system, meaning that even without a traditional gaming GPU, AAA titles are on the table.\nRTX Spark Dev Box\nJoined on stage by Nvidia’s Jensen Huang and Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella, President of Windows and Devices Pavan Davuluri also unveiled the\nSurface RTX Spark Dev Box.\nThis is a $5,999 anodized aluminum black box “designed to meet the needs of frontier developers.” The Spark Dev Box ships with a custom “developer-optimized” build of Windows 11 pre-configured for AI development and local AI use. With 128GB of unified memory and the onboard Nvidia RTX GPU, the box claims one petaflop (that’s 1,000 teraflops) of AI compute performance.\nThe RTX Spark Dev Box’s design is quite sleek. The unit physically resembles something like an Android TV box or game console more than it does a traditional PC. In fact, the vent pattern on the top of the unit is reminiscent of what you see looking down at the top of an Xbox Series X. According to Microsoft’s provided press materials, the venting on the RTX Spark Dev box has 1,000 chassis air vents as a nod to the 1,000 teraflops of AI performance.\nLocal agentic AI is coming to Windows\nOutside of the hardware announcements, Microsoft outlined a host of changes headed for Windows 11 this fall. OS builds featured on the Surface Ultra and Spark Box will arrive with an updated Windows software suite to support local model testing out of the box.\nCopilot overhaul\nMicrosoft’s baked-in Windows AI, Copilot, is getting a major overhaul. The new Copilot layout will see things divided into three tabs:\nHome\n– A starting place where users can interact with LLM models\nCode\n– The space where Microsoft claims that users with no previous coding experience will be able to prompt their way to developing widgets and Windows applications.\nAutopilot\n– The tab for interacting with AI agents. This is where users will oversee their agentic bot tasks.\nNew Windows Search Experience\nComing to a Windows PC later this year, the new update will allow Windows users to execute system-level tasks directly from the search bar. Rather than opening up something like Control Panel to adjust display settings, the user will presumably be able to describe the action they want and have the system execute it directly from the taskbar tray.\nMicrosoft Execution Containers\nFor those who might still feel a little trepidation about turning over their entire digital lives and files to AI bots, Microsoft announced the availability of Microsoft Execution Containers (MXC), a security framework designed to keep agent activities confined to a sandbox. Available to developers today, the framework allows system admins and organizations to monitor active agents working within their systems and to specifically set guardrails for each, ensuring control over exactly what each agent is able to access.\nMXC acts as a policy layer\nthat directs compute traffic to the appropriate isolation backend. Depending on the workload, MXC can shift to a process container, session container, WSL container, or even an experimental hardware-backed isolation called MicroVM.\nThe hybrid approach\nBringing all of this new agentic workflow together with traditional Windows is a concept that Microsoft calls “Hybrid Intelligence,” which is a move away from cloud-backed AI solutions and a move toward local ones. Hybrid Intelligence will allow Surface Laptop Ultra or RTX Spark Dev Box users to allocate memory as they see fit for any specific application, as well as offer native support for local frameworks like\nllama.cpp\nand models from the\nDeepseek\nand\nNvidia Nemotron\nfamilies.\nWe’ll see if it works soon enough\nMicrosoft is betting big on local AI with the changes announced today, but questions about security and user acceptance still remain, especially in light of their past missteps and other\nrecent AI agent mishaps\n. The Surface Laptop Ultra hits shelves next week—keep an eye out for our review, and we’ll try to figure out whether it’s worth the $2,599 entry price and what this local AI capability buys you.\nNick Indge\nSenior Technology Reporter\nNick Indge\nSenior Technology Reporter\nNick Indge is a Senior Technology Reporter at Ars Technica covering hardware, embedded systems, and self-hosted infrastructure. A former security investigator turned tech writer, he writes to help readers escape subscription traps and regain ownership of their digital lives. He lives in Chicagoland with his family and a goofball husky.\n65 Comments", "fetched_at": "2026-10-08T09:28:06.654909", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "マイクロソフト", "s": 0.125, "e": 1.0, "sm": 125, "em": 1000, "cs": 0, "ce": 7, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "は", "s": 1.012, "e": 1.137, "sm": 1012, "em": 1137, "cs": 7, "ce": 8, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "新しい", "s": 1.15, "e": 1.837, "sm": 1150, "em": 1837, "cs": 8, "ce": 11, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "「", "s": 1.837, "e": 2.062, "sm": 1837, "em": 2062, "cs": 11, "ce": 12, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "Surface", "s": 2.062, "e": 2.675, "sm": 2062, "em": 2675, "cs": 12, "ce": 19, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "Laptop", "s": 2.688, "e": 3.25, "sm": 2687, "em": 3250, "cs": 20, "ce": 26, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "Ultra", "s": 3.263, "e": 3.725, "sm": 3262, "em": 3725, "cs": 27, "ce": 32, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "」", "s": 3.725, "e": 3.75, "sm": 3725, "em": 3750, "cs": 32, "ce": 33, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "を", "s": 3.75, "e": 3.85, "sm": 3750, "em": 3850, "cs": 33, "ce": 34, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "発表", "s": 3.862, "e": 4.35, "sm": 3862, "em": 4350, "cs": 34, "ce": 36, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": "しまし", "s": 4.362, "e": 4.725, "sm": 4362, "em": 4725, "cs": 36, "ce": 39, "si": 0}, {"i": 11, "w": "た", "s": 4.737, "e": 4.95, "sm": 4737, "em": 4950, "cs": 39, "ce": 40, "si"